.TH mknod "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
mknod \- make a special file or named pipe
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/mknod \fIfilename \fBb \fImajor minor\fR
.br
\fB/etc/mknod \fIfilename \fBc \fImajor minor\fR
.br
\fB/etc/mknod \fIfilename \fBp\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mknod -- make a special file or named pipe
Usage:	/etc/mknod filename type major minor
	/etc/mknod filename p
The 'type' is 'b' for block special or 'c' for character special;
'major' and 'minor' are numbers.
The second form creates a pipe with the given filename.
.HE
.B mknod
creates a new i-node named
.I filename
in the file system.
In the first form,
.I type
selects the kind of device special file to create:
.B b
for a block-special device, or
.B c
for a character-special device;
.I major
and
.I minor
give the major and minor device numbers that together identify the
device to the kernel.
In the second form (three arguments, the last being
.BR p ),
.B mknod
instead creates a named pipe (FIFO) called
.IR filename ,
and no device numbers are given.
.PP
The new node is created with mode
.BR 0666 .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
If the underlying
.B mknod()
system call fails,
.B mknod
prints
.RS
mknod: cannot create node
.I filename
.RE
and exits with status 1 -- typically because
.I filename
already exists, its parent directory does not permit the caller to
create files in it, or an invalid
.I type
or argument count was given.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mkdir ,
.B mkfs
